{
  "title": "Cabinet remembers your recently played games and surfaces them above the full grid",
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "id": "what-counts-as-played",
      "question": "Does a game count as 'played' the moment someone opens it from the cabinet, or only after they have actually played for a while (and if so, how long)?",
      "required": true
    },
    {
      "id": "per-browser-memory",
      "question": "Is it acceptable that this memory lives only in the browser on the device used — so the list is empty on a different browser, a different device, or in private browsing?",
      "required": true
    }
  ],
  "criteria": [
    {
      "id": "recent-section-above-grid",
      "title": "A recently-played section appears above the full grid, most recent first",
      "guidance": "With a history of played games present in browser storage, the cabinet page renders a distinct section positioned before the full catalogue grid, listing those games ordered most recently played first. Decision to approve: each entry links to exactly the same destination the full grid uses for that game, so a tile in the recent section starts the game the same way. Check by loading the cabinet with a seeded history and asserting the order and the link targets. The existing acceptance/recently-played.test.js is the check for this.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "replay-moves-to-front-no-duplicates",
      "title": "Playing a game again moves it to the front instead of listing it twice",
      "guidance": "Record plays for A, B, then A again; the stored history and the rendered section must contain A and B once each, with A first. No game id may appear more than once in the list at any time. No existing check can decide this on its own — cover it with a unit test alongside the store implementation; no gate binding is proposed for it.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cap-at-three",
      "title": "At most three games are remembered and shown",
      "guidance": "After recording four or more distinct games, both the stored history and the rendered section contain exactly the three most recent, oldest entries dropped. Verify with a unit test on the store and a render test. No existing check can decide this alone; no gate binding is proposed.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "survives-tab-close",
      "title": "The list survives closing the tab and returning later",
      "guidance": "History is written to a persistent browser store (localStorage or equivalent that outlives the session), not to in-memory state or sessionStorage. Decision to approve: a single named key holding a JSON list of game ids, newest first, with no server or account involved. Check by writing history, discarding all in-memory state, re-reading through a fresh module instance, and asserting the list is intact.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "unreadable-storage-cannot-break-cabinet",
      "title": "Unexpected stored data never stops the page opening or a game starting",
      "guidance": "With the storage key set to each of: invalid JSON, a JSON string, a number, an object, an array containing nulls/numbers/objects, and an over-long array — and also with storage access throwing on read or write — the cabinet still renders the complete catalogue grid, every game remains launchable, and nothing throws. Decision to approve: in any of these cases the recent section is simply omitted (or shows only the entries that were valid) and the bad value is replaced with an empty history rather than being reported to the user.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "retired-game-in-history-is-skipped",
      "title": "History entries for games no longer in the catalogue are skipped silently",
      "guidance": "Seed a history containing an id that is not in the catalogue plus valid ids; the recent section renders only the games that still exist, in order, and the page and all remaining games still work. Decision to approve: the unknown entry is dropped from the rendered list and pruned from storage; no placeholder or error tile is shown. Determine the real catalogue shape by reading src/catalogue.js rather than assuming it.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "full-grid-unchanged",
      "title": "The full catalogue grid keeps every game in its current order",
      "guidance": "The existing grid still renders every game in the catalogue, in the same order and with the same markup and links as before this change; games appearing in the recent section are not removed from or reordered in the grid. tests/cabinet.test.js and tests/catalogue.test.js must remain green, and the existing behaviour they describe must not be edited to fit the new feature.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "no-history-no-section",
      "title": "A first-time visitor sees no empty recent section",
      "guidance": "With no stored history (or a history whose entries have all been pruned), the cabinet renders no recent-section heading, no empty container and no placeholder text — the page looks exactly as it does today. Check by rendering with storage empty and asserting the heading text is absent.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "heading-reads-as-mine",
      "title": "The heading makes it obvious the list is yours, not a recommendation",
      "guidance": "A person reads the rendered heading and confirms it names the visitor's own history (for example 'Recently played' or 'Your recent games') rather than suggesting or promoting games. Taste call — approve or replace the wording.",
      "required": true,
      "human": true
    },
    {
      "id": "gates-stay-green",
      "title": "Existing lint and test gates pass with the feature in place",
      "guidance": "npm run lint and npm test both pass on the finished branch, including the pre-existing acceptance/recently-played.test.js.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    }
  ],
  "gates": [
    {"id": "lint", "run": "npm run lint"},
    {"id": "test", "run": "npm test"}
  ],
  "gate_bindings": [
    {
      "id": "acceptance-recently-played",
      "run": "node --test acceptance/recently-played.test.js",
      "proves": "recent-section-above-grid"
    }
  ],
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "recently-played-store",
      "brief": "briefs/recently-played-store.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Add a small module that owns the remembered play history: a read that always returns a clean array of game ids newest-first, and a record function that moves a replayed game to the front, never duplicates an id, and keeps at most three entries. It persists to a single localStorage key as JSON so the list survives closing the tab, and every read and write is defended so that invalid JSON, wrong types, non-string entries, over-long arrays, or storage that throws all degrade to an empty history instead of raising. Done means the module is unit-tested for dedupe-to-front, the three-item cap, round-tripping through a fresh instance, and each malformed-storage shape, and npm run lint and npm test are green."
    },
    {
      "id": "cabinet-recent-section",
      "brief": "briefs/cabinet-recent-section.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Render the remembered games in the cabinet above the existing grid. Read src/cabinet.js, src/catalogue.js, index.html and acceptance/recently-played.test.js first so the new section reuses the real catalogue lookup and the same link targets and tile markup the grid already uses. The section carries a heading that names the list as the visitor's own, lists the remembered games most recent first, silently skips and prunes ids that are no longer in the catalogue, and is omitted entirely — heading and container — when there is nothing to show. The full grid below is untouched: same games, same order, same markup. Done means acceptance/recently-played.test.js passes, tests/cabinet.test.js and tests/catalogue.test.js still pass unedited, and both gates are green."
    },
    {
      "id": "record-plays-and-resilience",
      "brief": "briefs/record-plays-and-resilience.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Wire the recording of a play into the point where the cabinet sends someone into a game, following whatever launch path src/cabinet.js and index.html already use, and matching the answer given about when a game counts as played. Then prove the shortcut cannot take the arcade down: with the storage key holding each malformed value, with storage access throwing, and with a history naming a retired game, the cabinet page must still render the whole catalogue and every game must still start. Done means those cases are covered by tests next to the existing cabinet tests, no code path in the render or launch flow can propagate an error out of the history module, and npm run lint and npm test are green."
    }
  ]
}
